From f88e47b33b0d0df0201d1f8a0fcbc614ad2f22ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 04:51:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 74/79] codec: mad: only throw frames away after a break that
 could have spoiled them

Three frames are dropped after every discontinuity, whatever the stream. That
is right for layer III, which carries part of a frame in the ones before it:
the first frames after a break decode against a reservoir that was never
filled and come out wrong, and no amount of care in the demuxer can recover
them. Layers I and II keep every frame whole, and the packetizer only ever
hands over whole frames, so there is nothing there for a break to have
spoiled - the three frames are simply 78 ms of the item thrown away.

An item looped with --repeat takes a break at every loop, so the head of
every pass but the first went that way. On a 4 s item whose first 60 ms is a
3 kHz marker burst, in a transport stream and in a program stream, the marker
came back once in four passes; it now comes back on all four, and the silence
the output plays across each loop falls from 0.22-0.56 s to 0.15-0.31 s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 modules/codec/mad.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/modules/codec/mad.c b/modules/codec/mad.c
index 37eb9c4d8a..536b409a84 100644
--- a/modules/codec/mad.c
+++ b/modules/codec/mad.c
@@ -144,8 +144,14 @@ static block_t *DecodeBlock( decoder_t *p_dec, block_t **pp_block )
         if( !MAD_RECOVERABLE( p_sys->mad_stream.error ) )
             p_sys->i_reject_count = 3;
     }
-    else if( p_last_buf->i_flags & BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY )
+    else if( ( p_last_buf->i_flags & BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY ) &&
+             p_sys->mad_frame.header.layer == MAD_LAYER_III )
     {
+        /* Layer III carries part of a frame in the one before it, so the
+         * first frames after a break decode against a reservoir that was
+         * never filled and come out wrong. Layers I and II keep every frame
+         * whole, and the packetizer only ever hands over whole frames, so
+         * there is nothing there for a break to have spoiled. */
         p_sys->i_reject_count = 3;
     }
 
